\begin_layout Standard
We now calculate the placement of the large polygons using a linear program. Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\P_{L}^*$
\end_inset

 to be the set of large polygons in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

. Note that, since they are convex polygons instead of circles or hyperspheres, we can compute an 
\emph on
exact 
\emph default
placement of the polygons with 
\emph on
rational 
\emph default
coordinates. For this, we use the following approach, which was also noted by Abrahamsen et al. in
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
after ""
key "abrahamsen2020framework"
literal "false"

\end_inset

. Consider the placement of the polygons 
\begin_inset Formula $\P_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

. Each side 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 of each polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}\in\P_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is contained in a line 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x :a_{e}x=b_{e}\}$
\end_inset

 for some vector 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{e}$
\end_inset

 and a scalar 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{e}$
\end_inset

. For each corner vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 of each polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{j}\in\P_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we have that 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{e}x\ge b_{e}$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{e}x\le b_{e}$
\end_inset

 (or both); we guess which of these cases applies. Let 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a special vertex for each polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_i$
\end_inset

 defined as a vertex with the least value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{(1)}$
\end_inset

. Then, the coordinates 
\begin_inset Formula $({x}_{i}^{(1)},{x}_{i}^{(2)})$
\end_inset

 of the special vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 of each polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}\in\P_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 satisfy a system of linear inequalities defined as follows. There are three types of inequalities: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Positivity constraints
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)} \ge0, \forall P_{i}\in\P_{L}^{*}\nonumber$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Packing constraints
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall P_{i},P_{j} \in \P_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 any vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 in polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{j} \neq P_{i}$
\end_inset

 cannot lie inside 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}$
\end_inset

. A vertex 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 lies inside polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}$
\end_inset

 if it satisfies the inequalities described above.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\emph on
Container constraints
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall P_{i}\in\P_{L}^{*}\nonumber$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $0 \leq x_{i}^{(1)} \leq a_i$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $b_i \leq x_{i}^{(2)} \leq 1-c_i$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_i, b_i$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $c_i$
\end_inset

 can be calculated exactly in constant time for a given polygon. They represent the maximum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,v}^{(1)}-x_{i}^{(1)}$
\end_inset

, maximum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{(2)}-x_{i,v}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

, and maximum value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,v}^{(2)}-x_{i}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i,v}^{(1)},x_{i,v}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

 vary over all vertices 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 of polygon 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

